C = [
("h1", "2. The Grounds the Nations Are Charged With"),
("p", "Claim: the oracles against the nations give reasons, and the reasons are of a few kinds that recur from prophet to prophet: violence done to the helpless, betrayal of those bound to the nation by kinship or covenant, gloating over Israel’s fall, self-exaltation, and idols. The charges are brought against nations that were never given Israel’s Law, and not one names a statute of Sinai. They name what the nations did to other people and what they said of themselves. That is the first fact about the oracles, and it is the one that makes the rest of the volume possible: the LORD judges the nations on a ground he states."),
("sub", "2.1 Violence done to the helpless"),
("p", "Amos 1:3 to 2:3 is the plainest list. The formula is the same six times: “For three transgressions of Damascus, and for four, I will not turn away the punishment thereof,” and then a reason introduced by “because.” Damascus: “because they have threshed Gilead with threshing instruments of iron” (Amos 1:3). Ammon: “because they have ripped up the women with child of Gilead, that they might enlarge their border” (Amos 1:13). Moab: “because he burned the bones of the king of Edom into lime” (Amos 2:1). The three charges have one shape. A people with an army did to a people without one what the strong may do to the weak, and the last of the three did it to the dead. The reason Ammon gave was “that they might enlarge their border,” which names a nation’s ordinary ambition as the thing charged."),
("p", "Habakkuk’s taunt has the same ground in a different voice. “Shall not all these take up a parable against him, and a taunting proverb against him, and say, Woe to him that increaseth that which is not his! how long?” (Habakkuk 2:6). The proud man “hast spoiled many nations” and the sentence follows from it: “all the remnant of the people shall spoil thee; because of men’s blood, and for the violence of the land, of the city, and of all that dwell therein” (Habakkuk 2:8). The refrain returns at the end of the stanzas: “For the violence of Lebanon shall cover thee, and the spoil of beasts, which made them afraid, because of men’s blood” (Habakkuk 2:17). The woes name a town built with blood (2:12), a neighbour made drunk so that his nakedness may be looked on (2:15), and the greed that sets its nest on high (2:9). The ground is what the conqueror did to those he conquered."),
("sub", "2.2 Betrayal of the bound"),
("p", "Two of Amos’s six charges are against nations who handed over people to whom they owed something. Gaza: “because they carried away captive the whole captivity, to deliver them up to Edom” (Amos 1:6). Tyrus: “because they delivered up the whole captivity to Edom, and remembered not the brotherly covenant” (Amos 1:9). The phrase “the brotherly covenant” is the only place in the series of six that names a covenant, and it is a covenant between Tyre and a people it had sold. Edom’s own charge is of the same kind turned on a brother: “because he did pursue his brother with the sword, and did cast off all pity, and his anger did tear perpetually, and he kept his wrath for ever” (Amos 1:11). Obadiah gives the same charge at length: “For thy violence against thy brother Jacob shame shall cover thee, and thou shalt be cut off for ever” (Obadiah 10)."),
("p", "Joel names the trade in persons beside the nations he names. The LORD says he will gather all nations to the valley of Jehoshaphat and plead with them “for my people and for my heritage Israel, whom they have scattered among the nations, and parted my land” (Joel 3:2). He charges Tyre and Zidon and the coasts of Palestine, “The children also of Judah and the children of Jerusalem have ye sold unto the Grecians, that ye might remove them far from their border” (Joel 3:6), and the sentence is exact: “Behold, I will raise them out of the place whither ye have sold them, and will return your recompence upon your own head” (Joel 3:7). The ground of the charge is the sale. The recompence is the reversal of the sale."),
("sub", "2.3 Gloating over Israel’s fall"),
("p", "Ezekiel 25 is the chapter of the gloaters, and it states the charge in the speakers’ own words. Ammon said, “Aha, against my sanctuary, when it was profaned; and against the land of Israel, when it was desolate; and against the house of Judah, when they went into captivity” (Ezekiel 25:3). The LORD adds, “Because thou hast clapped thine hands, and stamped with the feet, and rejoiced in heart with all thy despite against the land of Israel” (Ezekiel 25:6). Moab and Seir said, “Behold, the house of Judah is like unto all the heathen” (Ezekiel 25:8). Edom “hath dealt against the house of Judah by taking vengeance” (Ezekiel 25:12). The Philistines “have dealt by revenge, and have taken vengeance with a despiteful heart, to destroy it for the old hatred” (Ezekiel 25:15). Tyrus says the same word as Ammon, in its own interest: “Aha, she is broken that was the gates of the people: she is turned unto me: I shall be replenished, now she is laid waste” (Ezekiel 26:2). Zephaniah collects the speech of Moab and Ammon in the LORD’s words: “I have heard the reproach of Moab, and the revilings of the children of Ammon, whereby they have reproached my people, and magnified themselves against their border” (Zephaniah 2:8)."),
("p", "Obadiah’s charge against Edom is the most detailed. It names the standing apart, “In the day that thou stoodest on the other side” (Obadiah 11), and says “even thou wast as one of them.” It names what Edom ought not to have done: not to have looked on the day of his brother, not to have “rejoiced over the children of Judah in the day of their destruction,” not to have “spoken proudly in the day of distress” (Obadiah 12). Not to have entered the gate, not to have laid hands on the substance, and not to have “stood in the crossway, to cut off those of his that did escape” (Obadiah 13, 14). Most of what Obadiah lists is not what Edom did with a sword. It is what Edom did while another was down. The volume leaves Edom there. The full treatment of Edom belongs to the volume that reads Isaiah 63 (Prophetic Commentary Volume 5)."),
("sub", "2.4 Pride and the claim to be more than a man"),
("p", "The second large ground is a claim. Moab’s is called pride in two prophets in nearly the same words. “We have heard the pride of Moab, (he is exceeding proud) his loftiness, and his arrogancy, and his pride, and the haughtiness of his heart” (Jeremiah 48:29), and “We have heard of the pride of Moab; he is very proud” (Isaiah 16:6). Jeremiah adds the reason for the fall: “Because thou hast trusted in thy works and in thy treasures, thou shalt also be taken” (Jeremiah 48:7), and “Moab shall be destroyed from being a people, because he hath magnified himself against the LORD” (Jeremiah 48:42). Edom’s is the same: “The pride of thine heart hath deceived thee, thou that dwellest in the clefts of the rock, whose habitation is high” (Obadiah 3). The sentence against it is stated in the figure of the claim: “Though thou exalt thyself as the eagle, and though thou set thy nest among the stars, thence will I bring thee down, saith the LORD” (Obadiah 4)."),
("p", "Two kings go further than pride. The prince of Tyrus is told, “Because thine heart is lifted up, and thou hast said, I am a God, I sit in the seat of God, in the midst of the seas; yet thou art a man, and not God, though thou set thine heart as the heart of God” (Ezekiel 28:2). The LORD’s answer is to a man, and says so: “Wilt thou yet say before him that slayeth thee, I am God? but thou shalt be a man, and no God, in the hand of him that slayeth thee” (Ezekiel 28:9). Pharaoh says of the Nile, “My river is mine own, and I have made it for myself” (Ezekiel 29:3), and Nineveh’s city says in its heart, “I am, and there is none beside me” (Zephaniah 2:15). The volume reads each as the claim of a king or a city to the place that belongs to the LORD. These verses are addressed to the prince of Tyrus and to Pharaoh. The volume does not carry them past the men they address."),
("sub", "2.5 Idols and the false helper"),
("p", "A fifth ground runs through the others: the nation’s god. Habakkuk closes his taunt on it. “What profiteth the graven image that the maker thereof hath graven it; the molten image, and a teacher of lies, that the maker of his work trusteth therein, to make dumb idols?” (Habakkuk 2:18). The final sentence of the chapter sets the one against the other: “But the LORD is in his holy temple: let all the earth keep silence before him” (Habakkuk 2:20). Nahum has the LORD say to Nineveh, “out of the house of thy gods will I cut off the graven image and the molten image” (Nahum 1:14). Jeremiah says of Moab, “Chemosh shall go forth into captivity with his priests and his princes together” (Jeremiah 48:7), and of Egypt that the LORD will punish “Pharaoh, and Egypt, with their gods, and their kings” (Jeremiah 46:25). The ground is not that the nations had gods. It is that they trusted them, and that the gods were made."),
("sub", "2.6 One observation on the grounds, and one text that widens them"),
("p", "The grounds in these oracles are the sort of thing the nations could know without Sinai: do not tear open the pregnant, do not sell captives to a brother’s enemy, do not exult over a brother’s ruin, do not call yourself a god. Amos shows the contrast in his own book. After the six nations he turns to Judah at 2:4, and there the charge is of the LORD’s own law, a ground the nations are not given. The volume draws one observation from this and no doctrine: the nations are charged with what they did to other people and what they said of themselves, and Israel and Judah are charged with what they did to the LORD’s own words. The same prophet who says it also says something wider."),
("p", "“Are ye not as children of the Ethiopians unto me, O children of Israel? saith the LORD. Have not I brought up Israel out of the land of Egypt? and the Philistines from Caphtor, and the Syrians from Kir?” (Amos 9:7). The LORD says he has acted in the history of other peoples as he acted in Israel’s. The sentence does not make Israel like the nations in every respect, for the next verse says “saving that I will not utterly destroy the house of Jacob” (Amos 9:8). It does mean that the oracles are not a quarrel between a god and strangers. The LORD claims the Philistines’ and the Syrians’ own movements as his work, and the oracles judge them as a judge judges the people he has dealt with."),
("ctbl", "The grounds at a glance.", [
    ("Ground", "Nations charged", "Passages"),
    ("Violence to the helpless", "Damascus, Ammon, Moab, the oppressor of Habakkuk 2", "Amos 1:3, 13; 2:1; Habakkuk 2:6–17"),
    ("Betrayal of the bound; the sale of captives", "Gaza, Tyre, Edom, the coasts of Palestine", "Amos 1:6, 9, 11; Obadiah 10; Joel 3:2–7"),
    ("Gloating over Israel’s fall", "Ammon, Moab, Edom, the Philistines, Tyre", "Ezekiel 25:3–15; 26:2; Zephaniah 2:8, 10; Obadiah 11–14"),
    ("Pride and the claim to be more than a man", "Moab, Edom, Tyre’s prince, Pharaoh, a city that says “I am”", "Jeremiah 48:7, 29, 42; Obadiah 3–4; Ezekiel 28:2; 29:3; Zephaniah 2:15"),
    ("Idols and the false helper", "Moab, Nineveh, Egypt, the nations of Habakkuk 2", "Jeremiah 46:25; 48:7; Nahum 1:14; Habakkuk 2:18–20"),
  ], [0.3, 0.32, 0.38]),
("sub", "2.7 What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "Every oracle in the series gives a ground, and the same few grounds recur across prophets and centuries.",
    "The grounds are violence to the helpless, betrayal of the bound, gloating over Israel’s fall, pride and the claim to be more than a man, and idols.",
    "None of the grounds against the nations names a statute of Sinai; Amos charges Judah, at 2:4, with the LORD’s own law, and the nations with what they did and said.",
    "Amos 9:7 states that the LORD has dealt with other peoples’ histories, which is why the oracles judge them as he does.",
    "The prince of Tyrus and Pharaoh are charged in what they said as men; the volume carries the charge no further than the men addressed.",
  ]),
]
